/// Used to compare different items (Task, Capability Command, Capability Activity, etc.). This is
/// accomplished by first comparing two ComparableRankingTypes' priority values. In the event of the
/// items having equal priority values the precedence values are then compared. For example, Task A
/// has a (priority, precedence) value pair of (4, 10), Task B has a (priority, precedence) value
/// pair of (4, 23), Task C has a (priority, precedence) value pair of (7, 2): the ranking order
/// would be first Task A, then Task B, then Task C. Since Task A and Task B have the same relative
/// priority value, Task A will be ranked before Task B because Task A has a higher relative
/// precedence value. Task C will be ranked after Tasks A and B because it has a lower relative
/// priority value than Tasks A and B.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct ComparableRankingType {
    /// Priority value of an item (Task, Capability Command, Capability Activity, etc.). Any item with a
    /// numerically lower priority value must be considered prior to any items with a numerically higher
    /// priority value. Priority doesn't necessarily dictate temporal ordering. A value of zero is
    /// highest priority, next highest is one, etc. Priority changes monotonically. For example, given
    /// values A=6, B=4, C=3, the order should be first C, then B, then A, with C being the highest
    /// relative priority. This element follows the priority ranking approach where lower values are
    /// higher priority and higher values are lower priority.
    #[serde(rename = "Priority")]
    pub priority: PriorityRankType,
    /// Precedence value of an item (Task, Capability Command, Capability Activity, etc.). Any item with
    /// a numerically lower precedence value must be considered prior to any items with a numerically
    /// higher precedence value. Precedence doesn't necessarily dictate temporal ordering. A value of
    /// zero is highest precedence, next highest is one, etc. Precedence changes monotonically. For
    /// example, given values A=6, B=4, C=3, the order should be first C, then B, then A, with C being
    /// the highest relative precedence. This element follows the priority ranking approach where lower
    /// values are higher priority and higher values are lower priority.
    #[serde(rename = "PrecedenceWithinPriority")]
    #[serde(skip_serializing_if = "Option::is_none")]
    pub precedence_within_priority: Option<PriorityRankType>,
}

