/// Represent the top-level envelope for all supported UCI messages.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub enum UciMessage {
    /// This message indicates the location and speed of a System or Resource. It is analogous to a
    /// simplified MIL-STD-6016 PPLI message. It is one of the "station-keeping" series of messages
    /// reported by most UCI Systems. The station-keeping series includes PositionReport, SystemStatus,
    /// NavigationReport, \[Capability\]Status and \[Capability\]Activity.
    PositionReport(PositionReportMt),
    /// This message indicates the current, highest level summary status of a System, its identification
    /// and an indication of the Services, Subsystems and Capabilities it contains. A System is anything
    /// that can self-report information about itself.
    SystemStatus(SystemStatusMt),
    /// This message indicates the current status of a service.
    ServiceStatus(ServiceStatusMt),
    /// This message indicates a Subsystem's current operating state. For configuration data use
    /// SubsystemConfiguration message.
    SubsystemStatus(SubsystemStatusMt),
    /// This message is used to notify an operator of something that may require their attention. It can
    /// be used to notify an operator that an error or issue is imminent; it is not assumed that this
    /// message represents an error that has occurred. This message is meant for a service to
    /// communicate information to an operator display. It is not to be used for machine to machine
    /// automation or to drive functionality directly. Notifications that require machine action should
    /// be given via other messages.
    OperatorNotification(OperatorNotificationMt),
    /// This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the ESM Capability of a Subsystem
    /// of a System. In general, a \[Capability\]Activity message describes what invoked Capabilities
    /// are doing. It is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for an
    /// entire Capability category (such as ESM, AirSample, EA, etc.). In other words, an Activity is
    /// the dynamic, per-use/per-invocation status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that
    /// includes 1) the autonomous (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the
    /// activity resulting from manual commands (ESM_Command) from an Operator or Capability management
    /// Service, 3) currently in-work ESM, 4) ESM scheduled for the future and 5) ESM that was scheduled
    /// and/or attempted but couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons. A
    /// single ESM_Command or other triggering event can result in multiple activities within the
    /// ESM_Activity message.
    #[serde(rename = "ESM_Activity")]
    EsmActivity(EsmActivityMt),
    /// This message is used to report a set of related Signals detected by a signals intelligence (ESM,
    /// ELINT and/or COMINT) Subsystem. The meaning of "related" is intentionally ambiguous. They could
    /// be related through a single common parent Activity, through their association to an emitter
    /// Entity or for other reasons. For signals intelligence Subsystems, detections can be reported at
    /// different levels of abstraction corresponding to the PulseData, SignalReport and/or Entity
    /// messages. Additionally, different C2 use cases can result in one or more of the messages being
    /// produced.
    SignalReport(SignalReportMt),
    /// A \[Capability\]Activity message describes what a Capability is doing. A
    /// \[Capability\]Capability message describes what a Subsystem is capable of doing if invoked; it
    /// is generally a complete, coherent snapshot of all Activities of a Subsystem for a Capability
    /// category (ESM, EA, etc.). This message indicates the combined in-work and to-do list of the PO
    /// Capability of a Subsystem of a System. In UCI terminology, an Activity is the dynamic, per-use
    /// status of a Capability. An Activity is an abstraction that includes 1) the autonomous
    /// (MDF-driven or MissionPlan-driven) activity of a Capability, 2) the activity resulting from
    /// manual commands (PO_Command) from an Operator or Capability management Service, 3) currently
    /// in-work PO, 4) PO scheduled for the future and 5) PO that was scheduled and/or attempted but
    /// couldn't be completed due to resource limitations or other reasons. A single PO_Command or other
    /// triggering event can result in multiple activities within the PO_Activity message.
    #[serde(rename = "PO_Activity")]
    PoActivity(PoActivityMt),
    /// This message is used to provide kinematic measurement data/products from a sensor, along with
    /// associated data collected in tandem (e.g., per-measurement radar cross-section data) with the
    /// kinematic measurements. This message supports measurements for both known and unknown objects,
    /// and may optionally be associated with a known object.
    ObservationMeasurementReport(ObservationMeasurementReportMt),
    /// This message indicates the location, speed, and direction of a System or Resource. It is not one
    /// of the "station-keeping" series of messages, but is designed to provide positional information
    /// at a much higher frequency than PositionReport, and allows for multiple redundant systems to
    /// provide reports in the same message.
    PositionReportDetailed(PositionReportDetailedMt),
    /// This message indicates the current navigation state of the System. It is one of the
    /// "station-keeping" series of messages reported by most UCI Systems. The station-keeping series
    /// is: PositionReport, SystemStatus, NavigationReport, \[Capability\]Status and
    /// \[Capability\]Activity.
    NavigationReport(NavigationReportMt),
    /// This message specifies the planned kinematic route of a System. "Route" is defined as a series
    /// of WGS-84 waypoints and is intended for Systems such as aircraft and ground vehicles whose
    /// kinematic plans are best represented in an Earth-centered rotational frame. Route planning can
    /// be done manually by operators, by a route planning service or a combination. This message
    /// decouples kinematic planning from allocation, action and other types of planning; the kinematic
    /// RoutePlan of a System can be created, updated and approved independent of other *Plans. It
    /// enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on route planning. A RoutePlan can be an input
    /// and/or output of planning processes. For example a coarse RoutePlan created by the top System in
    /// a hierarchical System of System for overall Mission orchestration and deconfliction could be an
    /// input to a route planning service that the produces a detailed, flyable RoutePlan for a specific
    /// subordinate System. A single System can have multiple independent RoutePlans for different types
    /// of planned routes such as primary, ingress, egress and contingency. The different types of
    /// routes can be developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented
    /// architecture at different times then independently updated and approved. Optionally, all types
    /// of routes for a System can be included in a single RoutePlan. A RoutePlan can be created
    /// discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. A RoutePlan can only be
    /// activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the execution
    /// order of multiple RoutePlans and *Plans in general. A RoutePlan can be subject to approval per
    /// Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state information for a
    /// RoutePlan are given in other RoutePlan* messages.
    RoutePlan(RoutePlanMt),
    /// This message specifies planned activities for a System with a RoutePlan. A "Route Activity" is
    /// any *Capability-related activity planned in context of the kinematic RoutePlan of the System.
    /// Activities are the planned implementation details of Effects, Actions, Tasks, Capability
    /// Commands, etc. Route activity planning can be done manually by operators, by an activity
    /// planning service or a combination. This message decouples activity planning from allocation,
    /// kinematic and other types of planning; the RouteActivityPlan of a System can be created, updated
    /// and approved independent of other *Plans. It enables any type of *Plan to be a constraint on
    /// activity planning. A RouteActivityPlan can be an input and/or output of planning processes. For
    /// example a RouteActivityPlan from a previous round of planning could be an input to an activity
    /// planning service that adds additional planned activities to it. A single System can have
    /// multiple independent RouteActivityPlans for different Task, Capability, sensor, etc. types; that
    /// are developed by one or more services across a multi-System service oriented architecture; at
    /// different times then independently updated and approved. Optionally, all types of activities for
    /// a System can be included in a single RouteActivityPlan. A RouteActivityPlan can be created
    /// discretely or as part of a MissionPlan that includes other *Plans. A RouteActivityPlan can only
    /// be activated and executed as part of a MissionPlan. A MissionPlan can be used to plan the
    /// execution order of multiple RouteActivityPlans and *Plans in general. A RouteActivityPlan can be
    /// subject to approval per Approval* messages. Planning, approval, activation and execution state
    /// information for a RouteActivityPlan are given in other RouteActivityPlan* messages.
    RouteActivityPlan(RouteActivityPlanMt),
    /// This message indicates the execution status for one or more RoutePlans. Plan execution begins
    /// after successful autonomous plan activation or activation via MissionPlanActivation messages.
    /// This message provides execution status regardless of how execution was initiated. *Plan related
    /// status is given in multiple messages aligned with the different types of Systems and Services
    /// that provide the status and the different times it is given. Those messages are *PlanStatus,
    /// *PlanApprovalStatus, MissionPlanActivationStatus, *PlanExecutionStatus; see their annotations
    /// for further details.
    RoutePlanExecutionStatus(RoutePlanExecutionStatusMt),
}
